I1017 10:42:59.803394    2951 input_loop.go:106] HandleUserInput called with text: "The Worker has stopped in step 8 (reported the final analysis; step 8 has no approval stage). This run is condition F: there are no issue files. The Worker's latest chat reply is pasted below between the markers. You may also read the code and data in the Worker workspace (read-only). Then show your review (Part 1) and your draft to the Worker (Part 2) in full in your reply, in the format of Section 4 of MANAGER_BRIEF.md (in condition F, every draft is a chat message), and append \"Manager memo 08\" to memos/thm01-R-gemini38f-r2-F/manager_memo.md as in Section 5. Append only: do not modify or delete any existing text in the memo file. Do not edit any other file.\n\n----- WORKER REPLY (pasted by the human) -----\n# Triviality Search Report: Inhomogeneous Deformation of $C(n+1) = \\mathfrak{osp}(2|2n)$ (Issue I08-1)\n\n---\n\n### 1. Executive Summary & Core Conclusion\n\nBased on exact rational linear-algebraic computation across $C(2) = \\mathfrak{osp}(2|2)$, $C(3) = \\mathfrak{osp}(2|4)$, and $C(4) = \\mathfrak{osp}(2|6)$, we establish the definitive triviality condition:\n\n> **Fundamental Result**:\n> The inhomogeneous deformation $\\gamma(\\mathrm{gb})$ of $C(n+1) = \\mathfrak{osp}(2|2n)$ is a coboundary **if and only if all deformation parameters vanish**:\n> $$\\gamma(\\mathrm{gb}) \\in B^2(\\mathfrak{g}, \\mathfrak{g})_{\\bar{1}} \\iff \\mathrm{gb}_{\\sigma, j, s} = 0 \\quad \\text{for all } \\sigma \\in \\{+,-\\},\\ j \\in \\{1,\\dots,n\\},\\ s \\in \\{+,-\\}.$$\n> In other words, **every non-zero inhomogeneous deformation of $C(n+1)$ is non-trivial**; the deformation space $\\operatorname{Im}(\\gamma) \\cong \\mathbb{C}^{4n}$ intersects the coboundary space $B^2(\\mathfrak{g}, \\mathfrak{g})_{\\bar{1}}$ strictly at the origin:\n> $$\\operatorname{Im}(\\gamma) \\cap B^2(\\mathfrak{g}, \\mathfrak{g})_{\\bar{1}} = \\{0\\}.$$\n\nThis stands in stark contrast to the base case $B(0,n) = \\mathfrak{osp}(1|2n)$, where inhomogeneous deformations can be absorbed by odd gauge transformations.\n\n---\n\n### 2. Case-Specific Evidence: Comparison of Layer 3 and Layer 4\n\nComparing the evaluated deformation constants $\\gamma|_{\\text{all\\_plus\\_one}}$ ([`data/C_1_evaluated.json`](file://<home>/notebook/git/thm01-R/thm01-R-gemini38f-r2-F/data/C_1_evaluated.json)) with the representative coboundary constants $\\delta f|_{\\text{mode\\_paired}}$ ([`data/C_1_coboundary.json`](file://<home>/notebook/git/thm01-R/thm01-R-gemini38f-r2-F/data/C_1_coboundary.json)) yields the following structural breakdown:\n\n| Metric | $C(2)$ ($n=1$) | $C(3)$ ($n=2$) | $C(4)$ ($n=3$) |\n|---|:---:|:---:|:---:|\n| Total Active Generator Pairs $(X, Y)$ | 58 | 321 | 974 |\n| Pairs in Evaluated Deformation $\\gamma$ | 54 | 296 | 882 |\n| Pairs in Coboundary $\\delta f$ | 40 | 185 | 392 |\n| **Exact Coinciding Pairs** | **4** | **12** | **24** |\n| **Differing Pairs** (active in both, different coefficients) | **32** | **156** | **344** |\n| **$\\gamma$-Only Pairs** (active in $\\gamma$, vanishing in $\\delta f$) | **18** | **128** | **514** |\n| **Coboundary-Only Pairs** (active in $\\delta f$, vanishing in $\\gamma$) | **4** | **25** | **92** |\n\n#### A. Coinciding Pairs (Trivial Alignment on Isolated Channels)\nIn $C(2)$, exactly 4 generator pairs coincide between Layer 3 and Layer 4:\n- $(E_{2\\delta_1}^+, E_{\\varepsilon+\\delta_1}) \\longmapsto +2 E_{2\\delta_1}^+$ in both $\\gamma$ and $\\delta f$.\n- $(E_{\\varepsilon+\\delta_1}, E_{2\\delta_1}^+) \\longmapsto -2 E_{2\\delta_1}^+$ in both $\\gamma$ and $\\delta f$.\n- $(E_{2\\delta_1}^-, E_{-\\varepsilon-\\delta_1}) \\longmapsto +2 E_{2\\delta_1}^-$ in both $\\gamma$ and $\\delta f$.\n- $(E_{-\\varepsilon-\\delta_1}, E_{2\\delta_1}^-) \\longmapsto -2 E_{2\\delta_1}^-$ in both $\\gamma$ and $\\delta f$.\n\n#### B. Concrete Discrepancies and Inconsistencies\nAcross all other channels, fundamental discrepancies arise that no odd linear map $f$ can resolve:\n1. **Boson-Boson Vanishing vs. Non-Vanishing**:\n   - For purely bosonic root generators $(E_{2\\delta_1}^+, E_{2\\delta_1}^-)$, the deformation bracket in $\\mathcal{U}(A)$ is purely governed by undeformed CCR $[b_1^+, b_1^-]$, so:\n     $$\\gamma(E_{2\\delta_1}^+, E_{2\\delta_1}^-) = 0.$$\n   - However, for any odd map with $f(H_2) \\ne 0$, the coboundary formula contains $-f([E_{2\\delta_1}^+, E_{2\\delta_1}^-])$:\n     $$(\\delta f)(E_{2\\delta_1}^+, E_{2\\delta_1}^-) = -f(-4 H_2) = +4 E_{-\\varepsilon-\\delta_1} \\ne 0.$$\n2. **Sign and Direction Inversions**:\n   - $(E_{2\\delta_1}^-, E_{\\varepsilon+\\delta_1})$: Deformation yields $\\gamma = +2 H_2$, whereas the coboundary yields $+2 E_{2\\delta_1}^-$.\n   - $(E_{2\\delta_1}^+, E_{\\varepsilon-\\delta_1})$: Deformation yields $\\gamma = +2 H_2$, whereas the coboundary yields $+2 H_1$.\n   - $(E_{2\\delta_1}^-, E_{-\\varepsilon+\\delta_1})$: Deformation yields $\\gamma = +2 H_2$, whereas the coboundary yields $-2 H_2$.\n\n---\n\n### 3. Rigorous Cohomology Analysis Across All Odd Linear Maps\n\nTo eliminate any dependency on the specific choice of test map $f$, we formulated the full linear system over the entire space of odd linear maps $\\operatorname{Hom}_{\\bar{1}}(\\mathfrak{g}, \\mathfrak{g})$:\n$$f(Z_j) = \\sum_i \\phi_{ij} Z_i \\quad \\text{where } p(Z_i) \\ne p(Z_j).$$\n\nFor each $n \\in \\{1, 2, 3\\}$, the dimension of odd linear maps is:\n$$\\dim \\operatorname{Hom}_{\\bar{1}}(\\mathfrak{g}, \\mathfrak{g}) = 2 \\times \\dim(\\mathfrak{g}_{\\bar{0}}) \\times \\dim(\\mathfrak{g}_{\\bar{1}}) = 2 \\times (2n^2 + n + 1) \\times 4n.$$\n\nSolving the exact rational matrix system $\\delta(\\phi) = \\gamma(\\mathrm{gb})$ via Gaussian elimination ([`src/C_triviality_analysis.py`](file://<home>/notebook/git/thm01-R/thm01-R-gemini38f-r2-F/src/C_triviality_analysis.py)) gives:\n\n| Algebra | $\\dim \\mathfrak{g}$ ($\\bar{0} \\mid \\bar{1}$) | $\\dim \\operatorname{Hom}_{\\bar{1}}(\\mathfrak{g},\\mathfrak{g})$ | Number of $\\mathrm{gb}$ Parameters | $\\operatorname{Rank}(\\delta) = \\dim B_{\\bar{1}}^2$ | $\\dim \\operatorname{Der}_{\\bar{1}}(\\mathfrak{g})$ | $\\operatorname{Rank}([\\delta \\mid \\gamma])$ | $\\operatorname{Rank}([\\delta \\mid \\gamma]) - \\operatorname{Rank}(\\delta)$ |\n|---|:---:|:---:|:---:|:---:|:---:|:---:|:---:|\n| **$C(2)$** | $4 \\mid 4$ (8) | 32 | 4 | 28 | 4 | 32 | **4** (full) |\n| **$C(3)$** | $11 \\mid 8$ (19) | 176 | 8 | 168 | 8 | 176 | **8** (full) |\n| **$C(4)$** | $22 \\mid 12$ (34) | 528 | 12 | 516 | 12 | 528 | **12** (full) |\n\n#### Key Observations:\n1. **Odd Derivations**: $\\dim \\operatorname{Der}_{\\bar{1}}(\\mathfrak{g}) = \\dim \\operatorname{Hom}_{\\bar{1}}(\\mathfrak{g}, \\mathfrak{g}) - \\operatorname{Rank}(\\delta) = 4n = \\dim(\\mathfrak{g}_{\\bar{1}})$. Since every basic classical Lie superalgebra has only inner derivations, $\\operatorname{Der}_{\\bar{1}}(\\mathfrak{g}) = \\operatorname{ad}(\\mathfrak{g}_{\\bar{1}})$, which confirms exact rank consistency.\n2. **Full-Rank Obstruction**: In every case:\n   $$\\operatorname{Rank}([\\delta \\mid \\gamma]) - \\operatorname{Rank}(\\delta) = 4n = \\dim(\\operatorname{span}\\{\\mathrm{gb}\\}).$$\n   This proves that **no non-trivial linear combination of the $4n$ deformation cocycles $\\gamma(\\mathrm{gb})$ lies in the coboundary space $B_{\\bar{1}}^2(\\mathfrak{g}, \\mathfrak{g})$**.\n\n---\n\n### 4. Mathematical Root Cause: Why $C(n+1)$ Differs from $B(0,n)$\n\n1. **Center of the Even Subalgebra ($\\mathfrak{so}(2)$ Abelian Factor)**:\n   - For $B(0,n) = \\mathfrak{osp}(1|2n)$, the even subalgebra $\\mathfrak{g}_{\\bar{0}} = \\mathfrak{sp}(2n)$ is simple and centerless ($\\mathfrak{z}(\\mathfrak{g}_{\\bar{0}}) = 0$). The auxiliary fermion satisfies $a_0^2 = 1/2$, and the deformation acts by shifting $a_0^2$ along the bosonic coordinates, which is isomorphic to an odd inner gauge shift.\n   - For $C(n+1) = \\mathfrak{osp}(2|2n)$, the even subalgebra is:\n     $$\\mathfrak{g}_{\\bar{0}} = \\mathfrak{so}(2) \\oplus \\mathfrak{sp}(2n).$$\n     The $\\mathfrak{so}(2)$ factor is a 1-dimensional central abelian ideal in $\\mathfrak{g}_{\\bar{0}}$, spanned by the $U(1)$ charge generator $J = H_1 + 2 \\sum_{k=2}^n H_k + (2n) H_{n+1}$.\n2. **Representation-Theoretic Incompatibility**:\n   - The odd sector $\\mathfrak{g}_{\\bar{1}}$ transforms under $\\mathfrak{so}(2) \\times \\mathfrak{sp}(2n)$ as the charged fundamental bifundamental representation:\n     $$\\mathfrak{g}_{\\bar{1}} \\cong \\mathbb{C}_{(+1)} \\otimes \\mathbb{C}^{2n} \\;\\oplus\\; \\mathbb{C}_{(-1)} \\otimes \\mathbb{C}^{2n}.$$\n   - The inhomogeneous deformation $[b_j^s, a_1^\\sigma] = -\\mathrm{gb}_{\\sigma, j, s} \\kappa$ introduces a non-trivial central extension between the charged sectors.\n   - Any odd linear map $f: \\mathfrak{g} \\to \\mathfrak{g}$ would have to map the $\\mathfrak{so}(2)$ charge generator $J$ into $\\mathfrak{g}_{\\bar{1}}$. However, $[J, f(X)] - f([J, X])$ produces a non-zero weight obstruction under the $\\mathfrak{so}(2)$ action that cannot vanish unless the deformation parameters are identically zero.\n\n---\n\n### 5. General Conjecture for $C(n+1)$\n\n> **Conjecture (Theorem on Triviality of $C(n+1)$ Inhomogeneous Deformation)**:\n> Let $\\mathfrak{g} = C(n+1) = \\mathfrak{osp}(2|2n)$ for any $n \\ge 1$, and let $\\gamma(\\mathrm{gb}): \\mathfrak{g} \\otimes \\mathfrak{g} \\to \\mathfrak{g}$ be the inhomogeneous deformation 2-cocycle parametrized by the $4n$ parameters $\\mathrm{gb}_{\\sigma, j, s} \\in \\mathbb{C}$.\n> \n> 1. **Triviality Condition**: The deformation $\\gamma(\\mathrm{gb})$ is a coboundary ($\\gamma = \\delta f$ for some odd linear map $f \\in \\operatorname{Hom}_{\\bar{1}}(\\mathfrak{g}, \\mathfrak{g})$) **if and only if**\n>    $$\\mathrm{gb}_{\\sigma, j, s} = 0 \\quad \\text{for all } \\sigma \\in \\{+,-\\},\\ j \\in \\{1,\\dots,n\\},\\ s \\in \\{+,-\\}.$$\n> 2. **Cohomology Embedding**: The linear map:\n>    $$\\Psi: \\mathbb{C}^{4n} \\longrightarrow H^2(\\mathfrak{g}, \\mathfrak{g})_{\\bar{1}}, \\quad \\mathrm{gb} \\longmapsto [\\gamma(\\mathrm{gb})]$$\n>    is **strictly injective**.\n> 3. **Non-Triviality**: Every non-zero inhomogeneous deformation of $C(n+1)$ defines an algebraically non-trivial, non-integrable deformation that cannot be gauge-transformed away by any change of basis.\n\n---\n\n### Completion Criteria Check\n- [x] **Comparison between Layer 3 and Layer 4 executed**: Direct pair-by-pair comparison completed and documented.\n- [x] **Specific evidence for trivial/non-trivial cases provided**: Coinciding pairs and discrepancies reported with exact fractions.\n- [x] **General conjecture on $gb$ conditions formulated**: Necessary and sufficient condition $\\mathrm{gb} = 0$ formulated and proved via full-rank cohomology analysis.\n- [x] **Automated Verification**: Integrated into [`src/C_triviality_analysis.py`](file://<home>/notebook/git/thm01-R/thm01-R-gemini38f-r2-F/src/C_triviality_analysis.py) and [`tests/test_C_triviality.py`](file://<home>/notebook/git/thm01-R/thm01-R-gemini38f-r2-F/tests/test_C_triviality.py) with all tests passing.\n\nAll 8 steps of the research project are now complete.\n----- END OF WORKER REPLY -----"
